{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 53bfe4d5f81043799398abef31d4760612bf5b18.\\n\\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layers are Ordinary Git storage and the Kernel checkpoint cache/serialization layer in docs/reference/architecture.md. The split between checkpointLimitWriter and checkpointChunkWriter correctly restores the documented 256 MiB serialized checkpoint ceiling, keeps compact checkpoint v3 and streamed verified reads intact, and does not change the architecture contract, so architecture.md needs no edit. The exact head nevertheless does not yet meet the request's cache construction bound.\\n\\nSECURITY. I reviewed untrusted and incompressible event payloads, attachment cloning, borrowed and owned-tail compression, streamed rebuilds, failure release, retry semantics, and output growth. The marshal ceiling, exact-capacity clone preflight, post-failure whole-limit reservation, and fail-closed read behavior are sound. One blocking peak remains in the chunks metadata. checkpointChunkSlotOverhead charges 64 bytes per stored chunk before c.chunks append. At a real [] []byte capacity growth from old capacity k to new capacity about 2k on 64-bit, the old and new backing arrays coexist during the copy and occupy about 24k + 48k = 72k bytes. Cumulative charge after precharging the new slot is only 64(k+1), which is smaller once k exceeds 8. The finished compressed output is also live during this append. Thus an adversarial near-limit history can cross a metadata growth boundary and exceed the promised construction peak even though the writer admitted it. TestCheckpointChunkSlotOverheadCoversSliceHeader proves only that 64 covers twice one slice header; it does not force or measure an actual backing-array growth boundary. Request changes: preflight and charge the actual old-plus-new metadata capacity at growth, or use a non-growing/chunked representation, and add a mutation-sensitive near-limit test that forces a real c.chunks capacity expansion. Also state explicitly whether the guarantee is logical retained capacity or physical allocator memory: cloneExact makes cap equal len but does not remove allocator size-class/page rounding, which the current model deliberately leaves as padding.\\n\\nSIMPLIFICATION. Splitting final-artifact size policy from cache peak policy is simpler and correct. The guessed flat slot constant is brittle; capacity-derived accounting or non-growing metadata would make the invariant visible and remove the architecture-dependent estimate. The abandoned-gzip path and whole-limit post-failure reservation defend the same cleanup invariant; retaining both is acceptable defense in depth, but the final tests should identify which one is load-bearing rather than imply both are required.\\n\\nGATES. At the exact clean head, branch equals origin. go test ./... -count=1 passed; go test -race ./... -count=1 passed; go vet ./... passed; targeted checkpoint cache/chunk/marshal tests passed; go test ./internal/kernel -count=1 passed in the parallel audit; git diff --check passed. These gates do not exercise the blocking real metadata capacity-growth peak.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:acb00817430ff0ed94a23050df25cc761552c644","head":"53bfe4d5f81043799398abef31d4760612bf5b18","verdict":"changes-requested"}}